Hi Biying,

I’ve now completed a substantial revision of the BERTopic manuscript and uploaded the new version to our existing Overleaf project.

Just to clarify the scope of my revision: your original manuscript, the R package implementation and API, the SMS example, and the original benchmark design and results are still the substantive foundation of the paper. My revisions were mainly to the structure and framing of the manuscript, and to make sure that the claims are supported by the evidence we currently have. I have not modified the package code or rerun the analyses, so I would treat this as our new working draft rather than a submission-ready version.

A few of the changes are fairly substantial. I revised the title and abstract to describe the package more precisely as an R interface to the Python implementation of BERTopic, and reworked the novelty argument to account explicitly for the existing `bertopicr` package. The paper now makes a narrower case for the contribution: the R-facing API, S3 object model, workflow integration, and validation of selected R–Python input/output mappings. I also removed language that could be read as suggesting that the package is an official implementation, a separate statistical method, or generally superior to other R interfaces.

I also made the backend-version issue explicit. The current package and benchmark use Python BERTopic 0.16.0, while the current upstream release is 0.17.4, so we should not claim forward compatibility until we have actually tested it. For the worked example, I reframed the SMS analysis as a demonstration of the software workflow rather than a validation of a behavioral construct, documented the composition of the 2,247-message subset, corrected the heatmap example to use the exported R wrapper, and added a table with actual topic results from the existing benchmark outputs. I removed two static figures that were not closely tied to the worked example and revised the benchmark and Discussion sections to be more precise about what we can conclude regarding efficiency, topic quality, API coverage, and behavioral validity.

The next round will need to focus on the software itself and the provenance of the results. Could you first read through the revised manuscript and make sure that my descriptions of the package and API are technically correct?

After that, please work through the issues identified in the software audit. In particular, please check the representative-documents wrapper, `nr_topics` handling in topic reduction, synchronization of the R-side `topics` and `probs` fields after changes to the Python model, Python key types for custom topic labels, and the mapping between document–topic matrix columns and actual topic IDs. The documented self-check should also perform the round trip it says it does, and save/load should restore enough R-side state for a loaded model to behave consistently. Please also fix any tests that currently turn failures into skips or inadvertently mask an exported function.

Once those issues are resolved, please decide and document the Python BERTopic version range we actually support, make the Conda and virtual-environment installation routes consistent, report the exact dependency versions, and run clean tests on macOS, Linux, and Windows. We should then make a new versioned package release and rerun both the worked example and the direct R-versus-Python benchmark using that exact release. Since the current numerical results come from the original benchmark, I don’t think we should carry them over automatically after making changes to the software. Please keep the scripts, raw outputs, environment files, seeds, hashes, process settings, and regenerated figures from the rerun.

There is also one data issue we need to resolve. The packaged SMS data contain all 747 spam messages but only 1,500 of the 4,827 ham messages in the original collection. Please see if you can determine how those 1,500 messages were selected and document the selection rule and random seed, if there was one. We should also have a script that reconstructs the packaged dataset from the UCI source. If the original selection cannot be reconstructed, that is fine—we can replace it with a transparent deterministic subset and rerun the analyses.

Once the software and results are stable, please put together a versioned OSF or Zenodo archive with the release source, reconstruction and analysis scripts, raw benchmark results, tables and figures, environment files, and manuscript source. I’ll also need the archive DOI, both affiliations, corresponding-author information, funding and competing-interests statements, and a proposed CRediT contribution statement. The manuscript authorship and package-development credits do not necessarily need to be identical, but we should make sure everyone’s role is clear and intentional.

When you have the corrected release, rerun outputs, archive information, and author details ready, send them to me and I’ll go through the new results, revise the manuscript as needed, and do the final journal-format and submission-level edit. For now, I would focus your time on the software corrections, reproducibility work, rerunning the analyses, and filling in the missing factual information rather than further stylistic edits to the manuscript.

